  "explain": {
   "text": "To compare two fractions, count both in the same piece. 3/8 against 5/16: in sixteenths, 3/8 is 6/16. Six against five, so 3/8 is bigger, by one sixteenth of an inch. A shared bottom like this is a [[common denominator]].[^1]\n\nThe trap: 5/16 looks bigger because 5 beats 3 and 16 beats 8.[^2] But its 5 counts smaller pieces: 0.3125 against 0.375.\n\n**Match the piece first; then the bigger top is the bigger fraction.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
   "text": "A 500 mA fuse and a 3 A fuse: 500 beats 3 as bare numbers, but in milliamps it is 500 against 3000.",
   "breaks": "Units fix the piece for you; with fractions you choose it, and it must measure both."
  },
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** same piece first, then compare the tops.\n\n**Worked example:** the limit is 3/8 inch and the bit is stamped 11/32. 32 ÷ 8 = 4, so 3/8 is 12/32. 11 is under 12: the bit is within the limit.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Comparing tops while the bottoms differ.\n- Choosing a piece that measures only one fraction."
  },
